 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 33,
   "y": 26,
   "text": "THE SOUTH DOWNS WAY. West to Winchester, 100 miles. East to Eastbourne, not quite so far. South to Brighton, downhill all the way, which is the best direction for a hill."
  },
  {
   "x": 33,
   "y": 23,
   "text": "At 248 metres this is the highest point in East Sussex. A fire beacon stood here in the chain of warning lights lit when the Spanish Armada sailed in 1588. On the London to Brighton bike ride, this is the hill everyone talks about afterwards.",
   "plaque": "southdowns_ditchling",
   "title": "Ditchling Beacon"
  },
  {
   "x": 36,
   "y": 24,
   "text": "Chalk lets rain straight through, so downland farmers dug shallow ponds lined with puddled clay and straw to catch it for their sheep. They are called dew ponds, though it is mostly rain that fills them. Some are hundreds of years old.",
   "plaque": "southdowns_dew_pond",
   "title": "Dew Pond"
  },
  {
   "x": 10,
   "y": 24,
   "text": "Two windmills above Clayton. Jill, the white wooden post mill, was built in Brighton in 1821 and dragged up here by teams of oxen in 1852. Jack, the black brick tower mill beside her, went up in 1866. Jill still grinds flour on open days.",
   "plaque": "southdowns_jack_and_jill",
   "title": "Jack and Jill"
  },
  {
   "x": 23,
   "y": 29,
   "text": "Skylarks sing as they climb, hanging over the downs for minutes at a time. The chalkhill blue butterfly lays its eggs on horseshoe vetch, and ants look after its caterpillars, milking them for a sugary drink. Close-cropped chalk turf can hold forty kinds of plant in a square metre.",
   "plaque": "southdowns_skylarks",
   "title": "Skylarks and Chalkhill Blues"
  },
  {
   "x": 55,
   "y": 26,
   "text": "A chalk figure about 70 metres tall, holding a staff in each hand. For centuries people took him for a prehistoric giant, but digs in 2003 dated him to the 16th or 17th century. In 1874 his outline was marked out in yellow bricks, and today it is painted concrete blocks. Nobody knows who he is meant to be.",
   "plaque": "southdowns_long_man",
   "title": "The Long Man of Wilmington"
  },
  {
   "x": 38,
   "y": 46,
   "text": "Chalk cliffs between Cuckmere Haven and Birling Gap, a row of summits and dips where old dry valleys have been cut through by the sea. The waves undercut them and the edge falls away a little every year, so keep well back. Some say there are really eight sisters, but nobody wants to be the one to tell them.",
   "plaque": "southdowns_seven_sisters",
   "title": "The Seven Sisters"
  },
  {
   "x": 56,
   "y": 46,
   "text": "At 162 metres, the highest chalk sea cliff in Britain. The red and white lighthouse at its foot was finished in 1902. The old lighthouse on the top, Belle Tout, was so often lost in low cloud that it was replaced; in 1999 the whole building was moved 17 metres back from the crumbling edge.",
   "plaque": "southdowns_beachy_head",
   "title": "Beachy Head"
  },
  {
   "x": 10,
   "y": 43,
   "text": "A spring-fed pond the locals swore had no bottom, home to a water dragon called the knucker. The name comes from the Old English nicor, a water monster. Lyminster's tale says a local lad killed it with a poisoned pie; a worn gravestone in the church, the Slayer's Slab, is said to be his.",
   "plaque": "southdowns_knucker_hole",
   "title": "The Knucker Hole"
  },
  {
   "x": 23,
   "y": 55,
   "text": "SOUTH TO BRIGHTON. Mind the cyclists coming the other way. They have been going uphill since London and they are not in the mood."
  },
  {
   "x": 30,
   "y": 29,
   "plaque": "arts_virginia_woolf",
   "theme": "arts",
   "title": "Virginia Woolf",
   "text": "Virginia Woolf and her husband Leonard bought Monk's House in Rodmell, under these Downs, in 1919. She wrote Mrs Dalloway, To the Lighthouse and Orlando in a shed in the garden, and walked the Downs most afternoons. In A Room of One's Own, from 1929, she put it plainly: 'a woman must have money and a room of her own if she is to write fiction'. She drowned herself in the River Ouse nearby on 28 March 1941. Her books are still arguing with the world, which is what she built them for."
  },
  {
   "x": 50,
   "y": 28,
   "plaque": "arts_ravilious",
   "theme": "arts",
   "title": "Eric Ravilious",
   "text": "Eric Ravilious grew up in Eastbourne and painted the Downs as nobody had: pale, dry watercolours of chalk paths, the Long Man of Wilmington and the Westbury white horse, with weather you can feel. He designed china for Wedgwood too. In 1939 he became an official war artist, and on 2 September 1942 the aircraft he was in was lost off Iceland on a rescue flight. He was 39. The chalk paths are exactly where he left them."
  },
  {
   "x": 36,
   "y": 32,
   "plaque": "landsky_knepp",
   "title": "Knepp",
   "text": "At Knepp, in the Sussex Weald a few miles north of here, a farm that had lost money for years was given over to nature from 2001. Longhorn cattle, Tamworth pigs, ponies and deer roam free and the land was left to see what came. Nightingales and turtle doves came, and purple emperor butterflies, and in 2020 white storks raised chicks in the wild in Britain for the first time in centuries. Isabella Tree told the story in Wilding, in 2018."
  }
 ],
